The signature moment is built and running. Report:

## What the moment is

**"How it falls" — a scroll-scrubbed chapter of a coat being put on, which does not cut to the shop but *becomes* the first thing you can buy.**

A garment does one thing a photograph cannot show: it falls. So the route opens on a tall lookbook plate held in ink, the reader scrolls, and ten real seconds of a coat being lifted, thrown across the lens, landing on the shoulders and settling play out under their thumb. On the way out the film does not fade — the frame contracts and flies down into the exact rectangle of the Wool Overshirt's image well in the shop below, arriving as the card's own photograph. Entry (plate → full bleed), body (the throw, the blackout, the landing), exit (film → product card) are all built.

## What material it moves

80 graded frames on disk at `public/media/fall/` (720×1280, 1.4 MB total) with a separately authored mobile set at `fall-sm/` (405×720, 684 KB), plus two chosen stills.

- **Source:** Mixkit item 41491, *"Stylish old fashion male model putting on a coat"* — one continuous vertical shot, one subject, one light. Free for commercial use, no attribution, no redistribution as stock; recorded in `public/media/CREDITS.md`.
- **How it was chosen:** 16 clips downloaded across Coverr, Mixkit and Wikimedia, 48 frames tiled into one contact sheet, then a second sheet of the winner's full 15 seconds to find the beat. Wikimedia has no usable fashion footage; Pexels/Unsplash need a key I don't have.
- **How it was treated:** trimmed to 4.000–14.000s, green cast pulled, saturation 0.62, contrast 1.16, vignetted, sharpened, resampled to 8 fps, then encoded per-frame at two sizes. The near-black stretch at frames 22–30 is the coat crossing the lens; I kept it deliberately and put the argument sentence in the dark.
- **Truthfulness:** it is captioned as representative film of an overcoat, "not a photograph of this garment", in the chapter and again on the card.

## What drives it

One authored value. The chapter's scroll progress, smoothed with a lerp in `requestAnimationFrame` — native scrolling is untouched, no Lenis, so anchors and keyboard paging still work. That single number picks the frame index, the plate's rectangle, all three type cues, and the ink ground's alpha. The exit rectangle is `getBoundingClientRect()` on the real card well, so the handoff lands wherever the layout actually puts it. Reduced motion gets a different authored still — the coat mid-air — in a designed still spread, not the sequence switched off.

Verified at 1440×900 and 390×844, normal and reduced motion, in Chromium. Console is clean, size selection and add-to-bag work by click, `npm run build` succeeds, nothing committed.

## What I could not get

- **No footage of these nine garments exists**, because the label is fictional. The film is a real overcoat, so the coat-shaped landing (Wool Overshirt) is honest; the same mechanism will need a different justification, or different material, for the tee and the shorts. That is the main open question for phase 2.
- Pexels and Unsplash both refused without a key, which cost me the widest photography set.
- The sold-out size treatment is written and styled but unexercised — the Wool Overshirt has no sold-out sizes, so nothing on this slice renders it.

Implementation of this slice complete; human taste verdict: awaiting your review.

